{
  "updated_at": "2026-09-30 13:20 JST",
  "total": 50,
  "toyota_summary": "Europe's car industry is fracturing over conflicting subsidies and regulations. Germany and France are deadlocked on emissions standards and local-content rules, while the European Commission weakened CO2 targets in January despite internal warnings against doing so. The split leaves manufacturers uncertain whether to invest in European battery and EV plants or look elsewhere.\n\nThis matters because a fractured Europe invites competition from China and Mexico. China is building out new telecom networks by 2030 as part of its industrial strategy. Mexico, meanwhile, is repositioning itself as a manufacturing alternative to Europe, with U.S.-Mexico relations improving. Automakers will naturally move investment toward jurisdictions with clearer rules and lower political risk.\n\nWatch whether Germany and France resolve their car rules dispute before the next EU budget cycle, which will determine if European subsidies can actually compete.",
